\section{Where the time goes}{

Only the search is parallel. Creating the result strings is serial,
because R's global string cache is -- the constraint \code{\link[=fsubstr]{fsubstr()}} also
documents -- and it is most of the work. So for a literal separator of
one or two characters the search is nearly free, base R is already at
that floor, and this function is no faster (on a 400,000-string vector
of short addresses it is about twice as slow). It pays off where the
search is the expensive part, or where the list is: a regular
expression separator runs about 2x faster than \code{\link[base:strsplit]{base::strsplit()}}, and
\code{simplify = TRUE} about 4x faster than \code{do.call(rbind, strsplit(...))},
which allocates every small vector before copying it away again.

Fields are cut exactly where \code{\link[base:strsplit]{base::strsplit()}} cuts them, including the
corners: a trailing empty field is dropped (\code{"a,b,"} gives two fields),
a leading one is kept (\code{",a"} gives \code{""} and \code{"a"}), an empty string
gives \code{character(0)}, and \code{NA} gives \code{NA_character_}. A \code{split} that can
match the empty string -- \code{"x*"}, \code{"\\\\b"} -- is an error rather than
silently doing something different from base R; \code{split = ""} is the one
supported case, and it splits into characters, or into bytes under
\code{useBytes = TRUE}.

With \code{simplify = TRUE} the result is a character matrix with one row per
string and one column per field, which is what \code{do.call(rbind, ...)} on
the list would give, but without the copying. Rows with fewer fields are
padded with \code{NA}, so a ragged split does not recycle silently as
\code{rbind()} would.
